---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'; import J from '../../components/J.astro';
export { paths as getStaticPaths } from '../../data/paths';
const { lang } = Astro.params; const ja = lang==='ja';
---
<Base lang={lang} page="projects" title={ja?'プロジェクト':'Projects'}>
<h1>{ja?'プロジェクト':'Projects'}</h1>
<article class="card" id="crm"><h2>jp-meeting-to-crm-fields</h2>
{ja ? <>
<J en="It reads Japanese meeting notes and fills in CRM fields automatically. It helps sales teams save typing time.">日本語の会議メモを読んで、CRM（お客さま管理）の項目を自動で入力します。営業の人の入力の手間をへらします。</J>
<p><span class="num">60.0%</span></p><J en="Exact match on 150 fields (30 test notes).">150項目のうち90項目が、正解と完全に一致しました（テスト用メモ30件）。</J>
<J en="What broke and the fix: tricky instructions hidden in notes could fool it. After adding defenses, the success rate of these attacks dropped from 45% to 6.7%.">こまったことと直し方：メモの中にわるい指示をかくすと、だまされることがありました。対策を入れて、攻撃の成功率は45%から6.7%に下がりました。</J>
<J en="Next: version 1.1, with per-field checks and cost and speed tracking.">つぎに：バージョン1.1で、項目ごとのチェックと、コストと速さの記録を入れます。</J>
</> : <>
<p><b>Problem:</b> Japanese meeting notes have to be copied into CRM fields by hand.</p>
<p><b>Built:</b> an extractor (qwen-2.5-72b, temperature 0) with a frozen 30-note eval set, JA/EN Streamlit app, Docker, CI, bilingual README, v1.0.0. About ₹2 per full eval, ₹0.03 per extraction.</p>
<p><span class="num">60.0% exact match</span> (90/150 fields, 30 synthetic notes), up from 28.7% across iterations.</p>
<p><b>What broke and how it was fixed:</b> prompt-injection text inside notes could steer the model. Defenses brought attack success from 45% to 6.7% on a self-written 40-attack set (3 runs), and from 30% to 10% on 20 held-out attacks (1 run). 162 tests.</p>
<p><b>Next:</b> v1.1 (in progress): optional LangGraph, a per-field judge, guardrails, cost and latency tracking.</p>
</>}
<p><a href="https://github.com/Retro099/jp-meeting-to-crm-fields">GitHub</a> · <a href="https://retro-jp-meeting-to-crm-fields.streamlit.app">{ja?'デモ':'Live demo'}</a></p></article>

<article class="card" id="rag"><h2>japanese-rag-eval</h2>
{ja ? <>
<J en="It tests how well search finds the right page in a Japanese government white paper. It helps teams check AI answers in Japanese.">日本の政府の白書（情報通信白書）から、正しいページを見つけられるかをテストします。日本語のAIの答えをたしかめたい人に役立ちます。</J>
<p><span class="num">0.98</span></p><J en="Of 42 questions, the right page was in the top 5 results 98% of the time.">42問で、正しいページが上位5件に入る割合は0.98でした。</J>
<J en="What broke and the fix: an older score could not be checked, because the original files were lost. I rebuilt the test and measured again. I also checked 15 answers by hand.">こまったことと直し方：むかしのスコアは、元のファイルがなくなって、たしかめられませんでした。テストを作りなおして、もう一度はかりました。15問は自分の目でも確認しました。</J>
<J en="Next: add more hard questions.">つぎに：むずかしい質問をふやします。</J>
</> : <>
<p><b>Problem:</b> it's hard to know whether retrieval over Japanese documents actually finds the right passage.</p>
<p><b>Built:</b> a 42-question eval on the 令和7年版 情報通信白書 (CC BY 4.0) using bge-m3, with 10 tests.</p>
<p><span class="num">hit@5 0.98, MRR 0.87</span>. On 12 harder questions, dense hit@1 was 0.83 vs BM25 0.50–0.67.</p>
<p><b>What broke and how it was fixed:</b> an earlier score couldn't be verified because the original PDFs were lost, so I rebuilt the eval and replaced it with a measured result. I hand-checked 15 of 42; all were correct.</p>
<p><b>Next:</b> extend the harder-question set beyond 12.</p>
</>}
<p><a href="https://github.com/Retro099/japanese-rag-eval">GitHub</a></p></article>

<article class="card" id="fraud"><h2>credit-card-fraud-detection</h2>
{ja ? <>
<J en="It finds fraud in card payments and explains why. It helps teams catch fraud with fewer false alarms.">カードの支払いの中から不正を見つけて、その理由も見せます。まちがいの警告を少なくして、不正を見つけたい人に役立ちます。</J>
<p><span class="num">0.84</span></p><J en="It caught 84% of fraud (recall 0.84), and 88% of its alerts were real fraud (precision 0.88).">不正の84%を見つけました。警告の88%は本当の不正でした。</J>
<J en="Next: explain the results more simply for non-engineers.">つぎに：エンジニアでない人にも、もっとわかりやすく結果を説明します。</J>
</> : <>
<p><b>Problem:</b> fraud models need to catch fraud without flooding teams with false alerts, and to explain why.</p>
<p><b>Built:</b> XGBoost with SHAP explanations, 8 tests.</p>
<p><span class="num">recall 0.84, precision 0.88, PR-AUC 0.88</span>. Per 10k transactions: 14.4 frauds caught, 1.93 false alerts.</p>
<p><b>Next:</b> plain-language summaries of the SHAP output for non-engineers.</p>
</>}
<p><a href="https://github.com/Retro099/credit-card-fraud-detection">GitHub</a></p></article>
</Base>
